                            Preamble

  The GNU General Public License is a free, copyleft license for
software and other kinds of works.

  The licenses for most software and other practical works are designed
to take away your freedom to share and change the works.  By contrast,
the GNU General Public License is intended to guarantee your freedom to
share and change all versions of a program--to make sure it remains free
software for all its users.

  When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not
price.  Our General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you
have the freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge for
them if you wish), that you receive source code or can get it if you
want it, that you can change the software or use pieces of it in new
free programs, and that you know you can do these things.

  Developers that use our GPL Licenses do not get a commercial license,
but the GPL itself states that you have the right to distribute copies
of the GPL licensed code under certain conditions.  However, the GPL
has explicit requirements for what happens when you distribute code
that links with GPL licensed code, which are designed to ensure that
the combined work remains free software.

  You can pay the copyright holder for a commercial license if you
wish, which removes these GPL requirements, but you can also distribute
under the GPL.  For example, the Linux kernel is licensed under GPL
v2, and many companies distribute closed-source modules that link with
Linux.  They pay for a commercial license from the copyright holder,
which gives them the right to distribute proprietary software that
links with GPL code.  This is a common business practice that
maintains the freedom of the Linux kernel.

  Once again: If you want to use our code in a proprietary or closed
source product, you can purchase a commercial license by contacting the
copyright holder.

  The precise terms and conditions for copying, distribution and
modification follow.

